(raster_dsl.emp:1224-1228) — the walk is over `fires`, the AUTHORED records, and `fire_lines` prepends the two priming lines separately (`comptime var out = [0, 1]`, :1252). So the pairs charged are (authored[0], authored[1]) ... (authored[n-2], authored[n-1]); the pairs (priming0, priming1) and (priming1, authored[0]) are charged by nothing, in a guard whose own doc calls itself the schedule-level authority ("Density is a property of the SCHEDULE — one fire cannot see its neighbour — which is exactly why the per-fire ceilings above could not catch it", :1210-1211).

Why it does not bite today, stated so the falsifier is explicit: a priming record carries zero ops and costs 286 cycles (raster_dsl.emp:1140-1144), `check_intervals` forces the first authored record's lowest reachable fire line to be >= 2 (`prev_hi = 1` and `lo_fl > prev_hi`, :1193-1198), so the smallest uncharged gap is 1 scanline = 488 cycles >= 286. The guard is redundant exactly as long as a priming record does no work.

That is a live falsifier, not a hypothetical: the priming pair is the one place in the format where a future frame-top action would naturally go (it already carried one — raster.emp:54-62 records the deleted per-program `init[]` register replay that `Raster_VBlank` used to run), and the preload change described in the finding above turns priming record 0 into a record whose successor is the first EVENT. Either change makes an uncharged gap the first thing to overrun, and the guard would report nothing.

