and :74-81 are its only three call sites, all self-tests: `ensure(variant_word($0888, variant(shift_r: 1, shift_g: 1)) == $0844, "Palette_DeriveVariant packing model drifted — the halve-RG test vector no longer packs to $0844")`, plus the bias-clamp and negative-bias vectors.

`grep -rn 'variant_word\|variant_channel\|clamp07'` across the tree returns those three ensures and NOTHING else — no data generator, no other comptime consumer, and (necessarily) no asm. The vectors prove that a six-line comptime function agrees with three numbers typed beside it. They cannot observe engine/effects/palette.emp:681 `pub proc Palette_DeriveVariant`.

The claim that they can is in the shipped code: palette.emp:677-678 `// branch-based so a channel can never overflow into a neighbour. The packing is / // proven at build time by the variant_word() ensure() vectors above.` and palette_dsl.emp:52-55 ("the asm in Palette_DeriveVariant must match them, and the ensure() below asserts a known colour through a known variant so the shift/clamp/repack is proven at build time").

FAILURE WALK. Edit the B-channel repack at palette.emp:730-732 from `lsl.w #8, d1 / lsl.w #1, d1` to `lsl.w #8, d1` alone (a one-line slip during any tidy of that unrolled body): every derived colour loses its blue. The build is green — the three ensures still pass, effects_budget_check does not read this, and the budget model records `compose_variant_derive_cycles = 0` (effects_budget_model.toml:161) precisely because the derive is gated behind PAL_ACT_VARIANT_STALE. So even a live OJZ boot (Pal_Active = $10, variant-only, no cycling — ojz_effects.emp:526-528) recomputes the stage image only on frames whose source moved, and the wrong derive stays latent until a fade or a cycle arms.

